        <h2 id="the-4-check-wiggle-test" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">The 4-Check Wiggle Test</h2>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          If we were standing next to your car, here's what we'd do. We call it the 4-Check Wiggle Test. The second the key moves, you're done.
        </p>

        <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Wiggle the wheel.</strong> Turn the engine off with the wheel cranked over and the steering lock can grab the ignition like a vise. So nudge the wheel left, then right, while you press lightly on the key. It lets go with a little click, usually.</li>
          <li><strong>Seat the shifter.</strong> Lots of cars won't cooperate unless the lever sits all the way in Park, and it's easy to leave it a hair short. Push it home. Manual? Try Neutral. A few models also want a foot on the brake.</li>
          <li><strong>Eyeball the key.</strong> Hold it up to the light. Is the blade bent? Is the tip rounded off, or the ridges worn flat? Then the key's your problem. Got a spare in the kitchen drawer? Go get it.</li>
          <li><strong>Peek in the keyway.</strong> Shine your phone flashlight in there. You'd be surprised how often pocket lint or a sliver of metal is what's blocking the pins.</li>
        </ol>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Still stuck after all four? Stop right there. This is the exact spot where people reach for brute force, and where a fixable problem turns into a real repair job.
        </p>

        <h2 id="common-causes-at-a-glance" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Common Causes at a Glance</h2>

