        <h2 id="what-not-to-do" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">What Not to Do</h2>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Pushing harder is everybody's first move, and honestly it's a bad one. Snap the blade off in the cylinder and you've got a chunk of metal wedged in there plus a longer job. That's the whole story behind our page on <a href="/services/broken-key-extraction/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">broken key extraction</a>.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Skip the oil spray, too. It grabs dust and turns to paste around the pins. A dry graphite lubricant made for locks is the better pick, and a pinch goes a long way. And don't pry at the steering column. You can wreck the column and the airbag wiring behind it.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Here's a habit worth breaking: the giant bundle of keys swinging off the ignition. Months of that weight chew up the cylinder, and it's a quiet reason keys start sticking.
        </p>

        <h2 id="worn-key-or-worn-ignition" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Worn Key or Worn Ignition?</h2>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          From the driver's seat they feel the same. The key catches, you jiggle it, and some days it turns and some days it won't. The spare ends the debate. If the spare glides in, your everyday key is the worn one. If the spare sticks the same way, the cylinder's the suspect.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          A worn key is the simple case, since a locksmith can cut and program a new one. A worn cylinder is more work. Our <a href="/services/ignition-repair/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">ignition repair</a> and <a href="/services/ignition-replacement/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">ignition replacement</a> pages spell out what each job involves, and plenty of them happen right in your driveway.
        </p>

        <h2 id="when-the-key-turns-but-the-car-wont-start" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">When the Key Turns but the Car Won't Start</h2>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Sometimes the key turns like butter and the engine still does nothing at all. That's the chip talking. Most modern keys hide a tiny transponder that tells the car it's okay to start. If the car doesn't recognize the code, it won't run.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Most newer cars have an engine immobilizer, a security system that keeps the engine from running unless the car recognizes the key's chip. The catch is that a damaged or unprogrammed key can strand you even when the blade is perfect. Want the details on the chip? Our post on the <a href="/blog/transponder-key-vs-key-fob-whats-the-difference/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">difference between a transponder key and a key fob</a> covers it, and our <a href="/services/car-key-replacement/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">car key replacement</a> page explains getting a new one cut and programmed.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Also worth ruling out is the battery. A weak one can pass for an ignition failure, especially on push-button cars, so if the dash lights look dim, try a jump start before you blame the lock.
        </p>

        <h2 id="who-fixes-a-stuck-ignition-in-dover-and-kent-county" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Who Fixes a Stuck Ignition in Dover and Kent County?</h2>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
